The lecture now turns. The same discussion that celebrates solidarity becomes a discussion of justice. A Japanese participant says that one problem with nuclear power is that the place bearing the risk is not the place receiving the benefit. This is the first clear asymmetry in the lecture: $\text{risk-bearing location}
\neq
\text{benefit-receiving location}.$ That relation changes the level of the discussion. A society may feel united, but institutions can still distribute burdens unfairly within it.

