p.src:
The analogy is then resisted, not by denying that airplanes can fail, but by insisting that nuclear power differs in scope and scale. This is where the lecture moves from unequal distribution within a nation to consequences beyond the nation: $\text{scope and scale of nuclear crisis}
>
\text{scope and scale of airplane breakdown}.$ The point is then made concrete: $\text{nuclear leak in Japan}
\Longrightarrow
\text{effects in China and America}.$ And from this follows the widened political conclusion: $\text{global effects}
\Longrightarrow
\text{world's attention and world's effort}.$ This is one of Sandel’s characteristic enlargements. What began as a question about a dangerous technology and an unjust distribution of risk now becomes a question about a harm whose effects spill across borders. The lecture has moved from domestic justice to transnational consequence.

