p.src:
Only after the lecture has widened the crisis does Sandel introduce a philosophical obstacle. He quotes Rousseau, who says that the sentiment of humanity evaporates and weakens as it is extended over the entire world. In the lecture’s formal idiom we can compress this as $\text{sentiment of humanity}
\downarrow
\quad \text{as it is extended over the entire world}.$ Rousseau is being used here as the philosopher of moral distance. It may be easy to care for those near us; it may be much harder to care in an equally vivid way for humanity in general. Sandel brings in Rousseau precisely when the discussion has become global, because now the question is no longer only whether a disaster has global effects, but whether our sympathy can genuinely match that scale.

